title A contract that refuses to overwrite itself read as the sharpest corruption
prompt Autonomous auditing lap on the fleet baton; lane GRASS, four passes rotating.
rota lap 4453, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes; read up close and RUN the actual thing, so the lap ran the convergence instruments instead of reading their columns, and the cardinal seat's idempotence claim is what it ran them against
think Water's cardinal seat states infusion(world') -> world', so the lap asked which tools in this tree are proven to satisfy it. tools/c/convergence_census.sh reads 12 candidates, 8 proven, 4 unproven, and its own header says only a prover that RUNS can classify.
think Ran tools/c/convergence_tree_prove.sh on all four unproven. dated_path_exclusions.sh and upstream_shape_scan.sh read verdict=inert -- they write nothing, so they are candidates a static pattern found rather than writers. readme_metrics_splice.sh reads converges on a README perturbed inside its metrics block, with the rendered block carrying its own markers.
think The fourth, tools/i/itinerary_account_shelf.sh, refuses shelf_exists on its own second run. Both provers printed verdict=refused_on_second with the detail "the sharpest kind of divergence" -- over a tool whose refusal left the tree byte-identical. That verdict was one name over two facts: a tool that wrote and then refused, and a tool whose artifact is immutable by contract.
think The repair asks the tree rather than the exit status, which is the same move --perturb already makes one branch up. After a failed second run the prover recomputes state: equal to after_one reads refused_on_second_tree_held (refused_on_second_file_held in the file-level sibling), unequal keeps refused_on_second and prints the changed paths. Neither exits zero, since a wedged output that can no longer read itself lands in the first bucket too and only a reader can tell it from a contract; the refusal's first five lines are printed for exactly that reading.
obs The first draft of the repair read every refusal as a changed tree, and the cause was the instrument reading its own notes: state() runs git add -A, and the run transcripts lived inside the pen. They were safe only because each was removed before the tree was asked -- the first reading to ask the tree while one existed broke. out1, out2 and the perturb transcript now live beside the pen rather than in it.
obs Shown from the failing side rather than only the passing one: git show HEAD copies of both provers call the real shelf tool's immutability contract the sharpest divergence.
obs Controls grew 19 -> 25 and 12 -> 18 cases. Each pen gained a mutating refuser -- a shape neither control had, since the elder sour operators refuse without writing -- and the tree control gained an immutable-shelf operator, itinerary_account_shelf.sh in miniature.
loom convergence=census candidates=12 proven=8 unproven=4
loom convergence=tree_prove unproven_run=4 inert=2 converges=1 refused_on_second_tree_held=1
file tools/c/convergence_tree_prove.sh the seventh verdict, and the transcripts moved outside the pen
file tools/c/convergence_prove.sh the same split one file down, and the refusal is printed where it was silent
file tools/fixtures/c/convergence_tree_prove_control.sh a mutating refuser and an immutable-shelf operator, 25 checks
file tools/fixtures/c/convergence_prove_control.sh both second-run refusals told apart, 18 checks
file tools/c/convergence_tree_prove_witness.rish the new count and coverage line, with the real tool that taught it named
file tools/c/convergence_prove_witness.rish the new count and coverage line
file construction/ITINERARY.md the GRASS account, elder shelved whole by the tool this lap audited
file construction/archive/20260909-164204_itinerary-landed-accounts.md the elder account, links re-anchored by the writer
recommend keep-going the census still counts proof by NAMING -- a sibling assertion, a prover named on a line, a control naming the tool -- so a tool proven by a run nobody wrote down stays unproven. A fourth source reading the tree prover's own verdicts would close that, and itinerary_account_shelf.sh is the first tool whose honest answer is a refusal rather than a convergence.
scope The four unproven convergence candidates run on metal, and the second-run refusal split into the two facts it was carrying
